# @NAME@

An editable shipping policy language. The grammar is in `reader.lang`; the
`@NAME@_check` function validates settings before lowering to shared AST.
`runtime.lang` owns target argument parsing and printing. The order total stays
a runtime input: the generated `@NAME@_cost` compares it with the policy threshold.
No compiler source changes are needed to edit this language.

The policy requires `base` and `free_over` once each, in either order. Values
are decimal whole units in 0..1000000. Orders at or above `free_over` ship free.
`policy.@NAME@` is positive; `syntax.@NAME@` and `semantic.@NAME@` must fail for
different reasons. `tests.json` records native inputs, exact output/exits and located negative assertions.
`cases.json` retains the comparison for the older minted-compiler checker.

Run the commands printed by `lang new language @NAME@`, from the directory
where you generated this project. They select LLVM/Linux x86-64 and use the
original tool's absolute path, so generation and checking work outside its repo.
Requirements: Python 3, Clang, its native linker, and a Lang source installation.
Keep the working directory here when loading the manifest. Runtime sources are
embedded by `project compiler`; the resulting compiler needs only guest source
to emit LLVM. Clang links that LLVM into the program.
The checker writes its LLVM and executable outputs under `build/`, keeping
guest sources intact even when the language is named `ll`.
Names must match `[a-z][a-z0-9_]*` and must not conflict with
compiler or reader toolkit symbols, such as `main` or `print`. Generated helper
names must also be free: `tok`, `parse`, and `scan` are refused because their
helpers would conflict with `tok_number`, `parse_check`, and `scan_number`.

After changing grammar, semantic checks, or the runtime, rerun `project check`,
`project read`, `project compiler`, and `python3 check.py ./@NAME@c` using the
printed tool path. Inspect `policy.ast` to see the comparison, and change
`ast_ge` to `ast_gt` to experiment with an exclusive threshold; update
`cases.json` to describe the intended behavior at 100.

Creation reserves a new directory and opens every file exclusively. An existing
destination (including an empty directory or symlink) is refused before writes.
If a later write fails, the command exits nonzero and leaves its incomplete new
directory for inspection. It never replaces or removes existing content; choose
a fresh name after fixing the storage problem.

Run the printed `test --project project.lang-project` command after edits.
Use `--fixture policy` for only the positive source. Changes to `free_over` or
`base` produce a visible test failure until `tests.json` is updated to match.
Receipts default to a fresh directory under `build/`; `--evidence DIR` selects
a fresh directory elsewhere. See the Lang language-project guide for the format.
